Do not write register contexts into minidumps.

Every thread record and the exception stream carry that thread's CPU context.
On x86-64 a context is the general-purpose registers plus the 512-byte legacy
FXSAVE area (XMM0-15), and more where XSTATE is enabled. Registers hold
pointers into, and fragments of, whatever the thread last touched, so on a
wallet they can carry key material that a crypto routine or a memcpy left
behind. The dump already carries no process memory, which leaves the contexts
as the only place a secret can still land.

Contexts enter the dump from two writers, and both have to stop producing
them:

  MinidumpThreadWriter       every thread's MINIDUMP_THREAD.ThreadContext
  MinidumpExceptionWriter    the crashing thread's context, in
                             MINIDUMP_EXCEPTION_STREAM.ThreadContext

Not calling SetContext() is not enough on its own: both Freeze() methods
CHECK(context_), which is fatal in a release build, so the handler would abort
and write nothing at all. The checks go, and Freeze() and Children() take the
context only if there is one. SetContext() itself stays, so a caller that does
supply a context is still served.

The records holding the location descriptors are value-initialised, so a
context-less thread serialises as {DataSize: 0, Rva: 0} -- how the minidump
format spells "absent", not a malformed file.

Backtraces are unaffected. They are walked at snapshot time from live memory
and written to kMinidumpStreamTypeSentryStackTraces by
MinidumpStacktraceListWriter under CRASHPAD_ENABLE_STACKTRACE=ON, never from
these contexts. The exception record (code, flags, faulting address and its
parameters), the module list, thread names and system info are all still
written. What is lost is the register view in a report.

The thread writer hunks assume crashpad-omit-process-memory.patch has already
removed the stack block. buildsentry.sh applies the patches in glob order and
omit-process-memory sorts first, so this holds.

tools/check-minidump.py asserts the result: every thread's context and the
exception stream's context are zero length.

Applies to sentry-native's external/crashpad submodule; see tools/buildsentry.sh
and ci/x64-windows/sentry.bat.

